<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="%%doc%%" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="%%dep_corelib%%" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <Patch Id="%%patch%%">
    <Canvas Id="%%canvas%%" DefaultCategory="Main" BordersChecked="false" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="%%app%%">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <CategoryReference Kind="Category" Name="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="%%app_patch%%">
        <Canvas Id="%%app_canvas%%" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="%%block_title%%" Bounds="50,30,900,200" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="VL.GeoJSON&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GeoJSON is the format most geographic data arrives in: plain JSON, readable, and understood by nearly every mapping tool. This package reads it and writes it. That is all it does - there is no geometry maths here, no map, no reprojection.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;What comes out is NOT a wrapper type. It is NetTopologySuite's own Feature and Geometry, which is the same language VL.NetTopologySuite and VL.Mapsui already speak. So data read here flows into either of them with no conversion node in between, and none of the three packages references the others.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Four nodes. If that seems few, that is the point: most of what you need is already in vvvv, and this package tries hard not to duplicate it.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="%%block_nodes%%" Bounds="50,260,440,540" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The nodes - all four of them&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Read GeoJSON   text in, the features out, as a Spread. Also tells you whether it worked (Success), what went wrong if it did not (Status), and how many times it has actually parsed (Parses).&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Split   one feature opened up: its Geometry, and its Properties as a Dictionary.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GetProperty   one property by name, plus a Success telling you whether it was there. Never throws.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Write GeoJSON   features back out as text. Exact Round Trip decides whether the coordinates come back byte for byte or get rewound to the RFC's winding rule.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Everything takes an optional Factory pin where an SRID is involved. Leave it unconnected unless you know you want something else.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="%%block_absent%%" Bounds="520,260,440,680" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="What you will NOT find, and why&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A FeatureCollection type. Read GeoJSON gives you the features directly, so there is no container to open first. That is how vvvv's own readers work: XMLReader and JSONReader take the document apart internally and hand a patch an XElement. A patch should never hold a &quot;document&quot;.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Because the result is a Spread, Count, GetSlice and a ForEach region all work on it - vvvv's own nodes, not ours. This package has no Count node for the same reason.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Read GeoJSON File. vvvv already has FileReader (String) [IO], with a Read pin so YOU decide when the disk is touched. See HowTo Read a GeoJSON file.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A node you can build from nodes you already have is a help patch, not a node. A node browser you can read is worth more than one with everything in it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;About SRID&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Geometry read here gets SRID 4326, because the GeoJSON standard says coordinates ARE longitude and latitude. VL.NetTopologySuite's Read WKT gives 0 instead, because plain WKT does not say what its numbers mean. Mixing the two never throws - it quietly gives a wrong answer - so if you combine them, use the Factory pin to make them agree. Setting an SRID is a label. It never moves a coordinate.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="%%block_next%%" Bounds="50,830,900,320" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Where to go next&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Read GeoJSON   start here. Text in one box, features out, and what happens when you break the text on purpose.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Read a GeoJSON file   the same thing from disk, and why this package has no file node of its own.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Access properties   the half of a feature that is not geometry. Two routes side by side - Split for all of them, GetProperty for one by name - and what type each JSON value arrives as.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Write GeoJSON   back out to text, and a round trip that proves nothing was lost on the way.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Handle a large GeoJSON file   the one that matters if your data is real. Watch a frame counter climb while Parses stays at 1.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Where geometry operations come from: not here. VL.NetTopologySuite has Area, Buffer, Intersection and the rest, and it takes exactly the Geometry this package produces.&#xD;&#xA;Where the map comes from: not here either. VL.Mapsui's FeatureLayer takes exactly the Features this package produces. Neither package knows this one exists, and that is the design working.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
        </Canvas>
        <Patch Id="%%create%%" Name="Create" />
        <Patch Id="%%update%%" Name="Update" />
        <ProcessDefinition Id="%%procdef%%">
          <Fragment Id="%%frag_create%%" Patch="%%create%%" Enabled="true" />
          <Fragment Id="%%frag_update%%" Patch="%%update%%" Enabled="true" />
        </ProcessDefinition>
      </Patch>
    </Node>
  </Patch>
  <NugetDependency Id="%%dep_geojson%%" Location="VL.GeoJSON" Version="0.0.0" />
</Document>
